{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 0.34,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 24, "low": 8, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 3, "low": -1, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 8, "low": 2, "param": "m"}
      ],
      "why": "Registering every m stages keeps 1 result/cycle (so throughput = Fmax, trivially clearing 50 MSPS for any Fmax>=50 MHz) while cutting flip-flop count versus full pipelining; the clock period stretches with m, so a large m is the natural way to reach the minimum-power corner. Search the whole m range, but expect the useful region near the top where Fmax is dragged down toward the 50 MSPS floor."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.26,
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 24, "low": 8, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 3, "low": -1, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"}
      ],
      "why": "The safest throughput family (1 result/cycle) and the most likely to sit at the high-accuracy end of the front; its per-stage registers make it expensive in power, which is exactly the trade-off we want mapped. Same accuracy knob ranges as the other families so the front is comparable across families."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.24,
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 20, "low": 8, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 3, "low": -1, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 8, "low": 2, "param": "k"}
      ],
      "why": "Shares the datapath but chains k micro-rotations per cycle; because both the cycle count (ceil(N/k)+3) and the clock period grow with k, its throughput is roughly flat in k and only clears 50 MSPS with a short iteration count. Give it a real share with small n_iter and the full k range, since if it can reach 50 MSPS at small N it is a serious low-power contender; n_iter capped at 20 to spend less time on designs that this family provably cannot clock fast enough."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.16,
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 16, "low": 8, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 3, "low": -1, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": [], "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"}
      ],
      "why": "Absolute minimum datapath (one shared micro-rotation, no unroll copies, no pipeline FFs) so it has by far the lowest per-cycle power and would be the cheapest feasible design if it could clock fast enough. Its throughput is only Fmax/(N+3), so I restrict it to small N and a modest eval share purely to check whether the 50 MSPS constraint is reachable at all; if not, it is the evidence for declaring that corner infeasible rather than guessing."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Throughput is the binding filter here: the spec demands 50 MSPS against a power reference computed at 100 MHz, and only the two pipelined families give 1 result/cycle unconditionally. iterative and unrolled_k trade cycle count against clock speed almost one-for-one, so they can only clear 50 MSPS with a short micro-rotation chain (small N) where accuracy is hardest to hold at 2^-12; I still fund them because if any such point exists it is the minimum-power winner, and if none exists the search will show it, which is itself a required outcome. Accuracy depends on (data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding) identically in every family, so I hold those ranges constant across families (W 12..20, N 8..24, angle_guard -1..3, frac_guard 0..3, both roundings) to make cross-family comparison meaningful and to span from just-below to comfortably-above 2^-12 error; W below ~14 and N below ~12 are expected to miss 2^-12 but are cheap probes of the constraint boundary. All four ranges are inside the registry and otherwise unconstrained. Budget is skewed to pipelined_m (34%) because the m knob is the only lever that lowers Fmax, and therefore power, while still satisfying 1-result/cycle at >=50 MSPS - i.e. where the min-power corner selected by the spec most plausibly lives - with pipelined (26%) covering the high-accuracy end of the front, unrolled_k (24%) probing a distinct datapath-sharing point, and iterative (16%) reserved as the feasibility probe for the cheapest possible architecture."
}

{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "rationale": "Constraints are reachable — pipelined (26 feasible) and pipelined_m (38 feasible) both satisfy throughput>=50 and err<=2^-12, so this is not infeasible. iterative and unrolled_k are dead ends (0 feasible; max throughput 14.6 and 9.04 MSPS, far below 50) and should not be re-explored. The front is still only partially mapped: power_index spans 2.01..10.1 against an HV reference of 40 (≈20% of the range), and accuracy_bits tops out at 20.9 while the best accuracy seen anywhere is 24.54 (pipelined_m) — that 24.54 design is absent from the front, so the true feasible accuracy ceiling is unconfirmed. With 200 evals left and the front covering only a small part of the power objective's range, the highest-value use of this round is a full-range NSGA-II sweep over the two families actually on the front (pipelined, pipelined_m), seeded with the current 15-design front, to fill the visible gaps (e.g. the 13.2→15.0 accuracy jump between power 2.64 and 3.13) and to establish whether accuracy can be pushed past 20.9 at throughput>=50. This directly targets hypervolume coverage rather than only the min-power corner the selection rule will pick."
  ,
  "next_plan": null
}

{

  "decision": "map_front",

  "next_plan": null,

  "rationale": "This is the last round (round 4 of at most 4, 100 of 400 evals left), which is exactly the reserved phase for mapping the front over the full parameter ranges of the families that actually appear on it. Evidence: iterative and unrolled_k are dead ends for this spec — 0/16 and 0/24 feasible, with best throughputs of only 14.6 and 9.04 MSPS against the >=50 MSPS constraint — so the front is carried entirely by pipelined (39 feasible, best power 3.42, up to 23.29 accuracy bits) and pipelined_m (52 feasible, best power 2.01, up to 24.54 accuracy bits). The feasible pipelined_m ranges are still narrow (m only 2..3, data_width 16..27, n_iter 14..30) while the registry allows m up to 8 and data_width down to 8, so the true front may extend beyond what we have sampled. The current front spans power_index 2.01..10.6 and accuracy_bits 12.3..23.3 with an HV reference of (40, 12); the +26.3% HV gain this round shows the curve is still being extended, so the remaining budget is best spent by NSGA-II over the full ranges of pipelined + pipelined_m, seeded with the 20-design front, rather than refining a locally-narrow region and risking leaving uncovered trade-off points. No next_plan is needed for a map_front decision."

}

{
  "rationale": "Budget and round cap are both exhausted (400/400 evals, round 4 of 4), and the feasible front is real and well covered, so `infeasible` is not warranted: 123 feasible designs (115 unique) exist, all constraints are satisfiable simultaneously (best throughput 163 MSPS vs >=50 required; best max_abs_err 4.11e-08 vs <=2.44e-04 required). The front spans power_index 2.01..10.6 against an HV reference of 40 and accuracy_bits 12.3..23.3 against a reference of 12, i.e. it already covers the useful part of both objective ranges, and this round's hypervolume gain was only +0.8% (394.9), indicating diminishing returns from further search. The selection rule (min power_index) is satisfied by pipelined_m [data_width=18 n_iter=16 angle_guard=-1 frac_guard=0 rounding=round m=3] at power_index=2.01, accuracy_bits=12.3 (max_abs_err 1.92e-04 <= 2.44e-04) and throughput 54.3 MSPS >= 50. The two non-pipelined families are structurally unable to meet the throughput constraint (iterative max 14.6 MSPS, unrolled_k max 9.04 MSPS, 0 feasible each), so no unexplored family remains that could extend the front. Stopping is therefore the correct terminal decision.",
  "next_plan": null
  ,"decision": "stop"
}
